  [1mB. Convert to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153midentity.js[39m exports named
  functions, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports them. Real boundaries, no load-order hazard, and the module is directly importable by a Node
  test runner. [3mTrade-off:[0m ES modules are blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops
  working — you'd need a local dev server. That's a genuine change to how you run this thing, and it's the main reason
  I'm not leading with it despite it being the cleaner structure. [3mWins when[0m you're adding real tests and more pages
  soon, and a dev server is acceptable.

  [1mC. Inline it in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, no module[0m — helper functions and direct [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m calls in the existing file. [3mTrade-off:[0m it
  directly contradicts "works across the app" — the second form would copy-paste or trigger the extraction you skipped.
  [3mWins when[0m the identity turns out to be single-page after all. I don't recommend it given what you told me, but it's
  the honest YAGNI option and it's the cheapest thing that produces a login record today.

  [1mRecommendation: A, with one amendment[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m takes the identity explicitly, [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, identity)[39m,
  rather than reaching for the global itself. That keeps [38;5;153mlogin[39m testable without a DOM or storage (you hand it a fake),
  and it means the parameter you originally asked for does get added — just carrying an honestly-named value instead of
  a [38;5;153muserId[39m the browser can't actually know. A also leaves the door open to B later: an IIFE module with a clean surface
  is a mechanical conversion to ES modules once you want a dev server.

  Two things I'd fold in regardless of which you pick, flagged now rather than smuggled in later: [38;5;153mlogin[39m is currently a
  synchronous stub that always returns success, and the real backend will make it async — I'd give it its async shape
  now so the call site isn't rewritten twice. And the capped log needs an explicit cap and eviction rule (I'd propose 50
  entries, oldest dropped) so [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m can't fill up.
